## Deploy

After CC commits and CI image rebuild:

````bash
# On agent-01
cd /opt/hp1-agent
git pull origin main

# Image change — wait for CI and pull the rebuilt image
docker pull ghcr.io/kbreivik/hp1-ai-agent:latest

# Recreate hp1_agent (pgbouncer untouched)
cd docker
docker compose --env-file .env up -d --force-recreate hp1_agent
sleep 10
docker compose ps hp1_agent
docker logs hp1_agent --tail 30

# Pgbouncer should now show fewer 'active' clients per page load and
# noticeably less long-running activity.
docker exec hp1_pgbouncer sh -c \
  'psql -h /var/run/pgbouncer -p 6432 -U pgbouncer pgbouncer \
   -c "SHOW POOLS;"'
docker exec hp1_pgbouncer sh -c \
  'psql -h /var/run/pgbouncer -p 6432 -U pgbouncer pgbouncer \
   -c "SHOW CLIENTS;"' | head -20

# Subjective: GUI should feel responsive again. /api/health fast.
curl -sf -w 'time=%{time_total}\n' http://localhost:8000/api/health -o /dev/null
````

If the GUI is still slow, the next thing to look at is hp1_agent's
sync psycopg2 path (`api/db/pg_pool.py`, `_PooledConnProxy`) — but
asyncpg accounts for the bulk of query traffic (the API request
path) so that's where the win should be visible.


